<p>
<font size=5>Education/Public Information News</font><br>
<b>UI Eco-Hydraulics Group (UI) and Linda Klein (LRK Communications)</b>
<p>
<ol>
<li>Steve Clayton and Peter Goodwin met with Kit Freudenberg at the Idaho Fish and Wildlife Foundation regarding sources of funding to enhance the educational facilities at the Red River WMA.  Kit agreed to keep the UI team posted if she learns of any potential funding sources that fit closely with the goals of the Red River project.
<li><a href="mailto:jabbott@uidaho.edu">Jason Abbott</a> updated the web page to include the August newsletter.
<li>Linda talked about our project with three classes of Grangeville 6th graders on September 4th as part of their annual Field Days.   She showed slides and brought riparian plant seedlings for them to see and touch.  They were a very enthusiastic audience and asked many good questions - future restorationists/natural resource managers perhaps?  
<li>As a complement to the current restoration activities, UI started two senior design projects:  a) Civil engineering students, in collaboration with Jim White (IDFG), are developing a design for a small portable bridge for pedestrian and ATV access for maintenance and monitoring and b) Mechanical engineering students are developing conceptual plans for remote control of cameras and data collection equipment at the RRWMA.  A future goal is to control an underwater and a surveillance camera from the Ranch House and to transfer current data and images to the project web page.
<p>
Outside funding is being sought to allow the fabrication and implementation of the bridge and purchase of the cameras and remote control equipment
</ol>
